    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "2bea8f30_8c2aae0b",
        "filename": "extra/usb_power/stats_manager_unittest.py",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 58,
      "author": {
        "id": 1165609
      },
      "writtenOn": "2018-07-24T11:01:06Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I wouldn\u0027t combine it with GetSummary as this is really only about accept_nan behavior, so we want to be able to detect if the summary stats are broken due to nan behavior or due to some other more general behavior.\nAs far as _populate_stats goes I can pull out the verification here into a helper that both of these call.\nBut the more I think about it, I actually would rather get rid of populate_dummy in favor of each test being self-explanatory. Thoughts/concerns?",
      "parentUuid": "6f32a656_a0f6c392",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 58,
        "startChar": 6,
        "endLine": 58,
        "endChar": 36
      },
      "revId": "951f4ab7ec56656640062fc475b1718b11fd8f1c",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "8883a1dd_6691a265",
        "filename": "extra/usb_power/stats_manager_unittest.py",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 58,
      "author": {
        "id": 1160860
      },
      "writtenOn": "2018-07-31T01:56:02Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I am ok with or without _populate_stats. I am ok with adding sample / unit in each test case. I care more about:\n1. using just enough sample for each test case, if you can use 3, then don\u0027t use 4.\n2. Using realistic and easy to understand sample / unit names. eg, sample A with unit mw. fake NaN with string \u0027fake_NaN\u0027.\n3. Samples across different test cases follow consistent naming. eg, keep using ABCD..., or test1, test2, ... don\u0027t mix up the naming. \n\nI\u0027m fine as long as code is concise and easy to read.",
      "parentUuid": "2bea8f30_8c2aae0b",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 58,
        "startChar": 6,
        "endLine": 58,
        "endChar": 36
      },
